{
  "title": "Applied Cryptography: The Math and Practice of Internet Security",
  "description": "The advanced follow-up to Internet Security Essentials. This course explains how modern cryptography works, from the mathematics up, and how to use it without falling into the traps that broke real systems. You will study security definitions, security levels and the birthday bound, modular arithmetic, AES and its modes, authenticated encryption (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305) and nonce misuse, hash functions, HMAC, HKDF and password hashing, RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic curves and digital signatures (with ECDSA nonce-reuse key recovery), the TLS 1.3 handshake and key schedule, certificates and Certificate Transparency, and post-quantum cryptography (ML-KEM, ML-DSA, hybrid key exchange). Every topic has formulas and a runnable Node.js script, including toy RSA, Diffie-Hellman and elliptic-curve implementations you can check by hand, and the course ends with a forward-secret secure channel built from standard primitives.",
